WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.720
For 17 seasons, NCIS' lead investigator Leroy
Jethro Gibbs has been building boats in his

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:10.690
basement, leading to some of the most memorable
moments in the series, as well as one of its

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:12.020
biggest mysteries.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:15.940
Obviously, the boats in Gibbs' basement are
very important to the character.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.439
On NCIS, Gibbs is a fairly straight-laced
guy, and boat-building seems to be one of

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.880
the few ways that he relaxes.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.820
Woodworking is an intimate, meditative activity,
and Gibbs' choice of pastime offers a lot

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.380
of insight into the character.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.020
"You're probably wondering, perhaps I rigged
it to explode."

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:33.620
"No.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.480
I was thinking, 'this is a really nice chisel.'"

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.980
But Gibbs' connection to his boats goes deeper
than a mere hobby.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:44.300
See, Gibbs has a habit of naming each of his
boats after someone close to him, often with

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:45.680
disastrous results.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.870
Sure, naming one of his boats after his second
wife, Diane, seemed like a sweet gesture at

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:50.400
the time.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.640
After the marriage fell apart, though, Gibbs
only had one choice: He had to burn the boat

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.440
to the ground.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.000
The boat that Gibbs was building during the
first six seasons of NCIS was a particularly

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.720
personal project for the Navy special agent,
as it was named after Gibbs' daughter, Kelly.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.219
In NCIS lore, Kelly and her mother, Gibbs'
first wife, Shannon, were killed in a car

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.080
accident after their driver was shot by a
drug dealer.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.159
The Kelly was supposed to be a tribute to
Gibbs' late daughter, but ended up meeting

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.660
a grisly fate of its own.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:22.340
In the season seven episode "Outlaws and In-Laws,"
the Kelly, which Gibbs finished off-screen

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.630
and gave to his mentor, Michael Franks, was
found drifting at sea with two dead bodies

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:27.240
on board.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.420
An ominous sign for sure, but thankfully,
the NCIS crew was on the case.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.540
"So what's the deal, what's with the dead
guys?"

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.980
NCIS is a show full of mysteries, as a procedural,
there's a new case to solve every week, but

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.020
one in particular has vexed fans since the
very beginning: How does Gibbs get his massive

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.760
sailboats out of his basement?

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.000
As far as we've seen, there's only one door
to the underground workshop, and it's not

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.800
nearly big enough for a full-sized boat to
fit through.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.680
For his part, Gibbs seems to like keeping
everyone in the dark.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.180
"How do you get the boat out?"

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.840
"Just break the bottle."

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:03.220
Another time, Gibbs referenced knocking out
a wall and building a ramp, but the logistics

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.760
of removing an underground wall remain unexplained.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.200
Occasionally, Gibbs answers questions about
his boats with his signature steely stare.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.200
Intimidating?

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:12.180
Yes.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:12.900
Informative?

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:13.900
Not so much.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.920
As a result, fans have begun coming up with
their own theories.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.950
There's some evidence that Gibbs builds his
boats in pieces and takes them outside for

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:25.340
final assembly, but even those smaller chunks
wouldn't fit through Gibbs' basement door.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.840
One wall of Gibbs' basement isn't ever shown,
meaning there could be an opening we've never

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.140
seen, although it would have to be awfully
big.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:33.320
The one thing we do know?

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.640
NCIS probably won't let us in on the truth.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.280
Only one other character, the fan-favorite
forensic scientist Abby Sciuto, has figured

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.520
out the secret, and, like Gibbs, she refuses
to tell.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:49.440
Officially, the Kelly was a Glen-L Amigo,
a 22-foot sailboat that Glen-L describes as

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:53.700
"a heavy boat capable of serious long-distance
offshore and coastal cruising."

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:59.280
According to Glen-L, NCIS' producers reached
out to the company directly about featuring

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.810
the Amigo on the show but required a very
quick turnaround time.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:08.379
That led to a flurry of negotiations between
Glen-L and the NCIS production crew, ultimately

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:12.400
resulting in the unfinished vessel featured
in NCIS' early seasons.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.420
However, when it was time for the Kelly to
set sail, production couldn't find a finished

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:21.320
Amigo to film, so they swapped in a different
type of boat and hoped fans wouldn't notice.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:26.500
Later, Gibbs began working on a Glen-L Barrelback,
which isn't a sailboat but a wooden motorboat.

00:03:26.519 --> 00:03:31.719
In the 2014 NCIS episode "Honor Thy Father,"
it's revealed that Gibbs and his father made

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.220
a small Barrelback named Chickadee when Gibbs
was still a child.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:41.000
After Gibbs' dad passed away, the NCIS investigator
secured plans for a similar boat and quickly

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.920
got to work, continuing Gibbs' tradition of
using his boats to honor members of his family.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.400
All of this suggests a question: will NCIS
fans ever get to see Gibbs sail away, perhaps

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.220
into retirement, on the boat he built?

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.520
Well, Gibbs is getting on in years, and operating
in a demanding, stressful job for quite some

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:58.180
time.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:02.859
In the season 16 episode "Lost Time," Gibbs
addressed the possibility of retirement with

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:05.870
his therapist, and he didn't seem too psyched
about the idea.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.440
"What's your endgame, anyway?

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.280
When will you ever be able to say, 'Mission
accomplished'?"

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.140
Gibbs had no real answer; he simply responded
that he owed too much to his team to depart.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.080
Harmon, though, has sung a different tune.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.000
In an interview all the way back in 2010,
the star said,

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.300
"From the beginning here, I always thought
that part of his manner in dealing with his

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.120
team is to prepare them for a time when he's
not here.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.340
So, I could see a time certainly in the future
where they take the job over and they move

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.710
on, and Gibbs gets on his boat and sails away."

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:37.620
That would certainly be a fitting end to an
iconic character, let's just hope his team

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:41.700
doesn't expect Gibbs, before he sails off
into the sunset, to let on as to exactly how

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.400
he got the boat out of his basement.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:47.460
We have a feeling that's just something that
they, and NCIS' fans, will have to keep on

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:48.140
wondering.

